  "result": "I fixed it: subcommands now get their own section of `default_map`, and the full test suite passes.\n\n**Cause:** In `src/click/core.py:392`, `Context.__init__` gave each subcommand context the parent's entire `default_map`. It should only get the nested entry stored under the subcommand's name. As a result, a subcommand looked up `x` at the top level and never saw `{\"sub\": {\"x\": ...}}`. It could also pick up the group's own defaults by mistake.\n\n**Fix:**\n```python\ndefault_map = parent.default_map.get(info_name)\n```\n\n**Test:** I added `test_default_map_subcommand` to `tests/test_defaults.py`. It checks two cases:\n- Group and subcommand each read their own `default_map` entry.\n- A subcommand with no entry of its own falls back to its parameter default instead of the group's value.\n\nThe full suite passed (1906 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed). I haven't committed anything.",
